WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.274
I passionately believe that

00:00:02.274 --> 00:00:04.866
strategic communication is critical

00:00:04.866 --> 00:00:08.240
to success in business and in life.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.364
But we must also

00:00:09.364 --> 00:00:11.179
think about strategy and

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:13.084
how we communicate that.

00:00:13.084 --> 00:00:15.388
Many of us focus on our day to day

00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:17.693
work on just the tasks we need to

00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:20.362
complete and executing them well.

00:00:20.362 --> 00:00:22.995
Taking time to reflect on strategy

00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:25.446
in our organizations and our own

00:00:25.446 --> 00:00:27.910
life can help us be successful.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.274
I'm Matt Abrahams and I teach

00:00:31.274 --> 00:00:33.188
strategic communication at Stanford

00:00:33.188 --> 00:00:35.130
Graduate School of Business.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.646
Welcome to Think Fast Talk Smart,

00:00:37.646 --> 00:00:38.693
The Podcast.

00:00:38.693 --> 00:00:40.141
I am very excited to

00:00:40.141 --> 00:00:42.790
speak with Jesper Sorensen.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.090
Jesper is a professor of

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.040
organizational behavior at the GSB

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.535
along with being the senior

00:00:47.535 --> 00:00:50.170
associate dean of academic affairs.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.295
Jesper specializes

00:00:51.295 --> 00:00:53.212
in dynamics of organizational and

00:00:53.212 --> 00:00:54.740
strategic change.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.742
He teaches strategic leadership,

00:00:56.742 --> 00:00:58.615
crafting and leading strategy.

00:00:58.615 --> 00:01:00.785
And along with fellow Professor

00:01:00.785 --> 00:01:02.794
Glenn Carroll, Jesper wrote

00:01:02.794 --> 00:01:05.286
Making Great Strategy: Arguing for

00:01:05.286 --> 00:01:07.146
Organization Advantage.

00:01:07.146 --> 00:01:08.117
Welcome Jesper.

00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:08.946
Thanks for being here.

00:01:08.946 --> 00:01:09.757
I'm super excited for

00:01:09.757 --> 00:01:10.486
our conversation.

00:01:10.486 --> 00:01:12.038
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me Matt,

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:13.927
it's really an honor to be on your

00:01:13.927 --> 00:01:14.538
podcast.

00:01:14.538 --> 00:01:15.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well, thank you.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.630
Let's go ahead and get started.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.098
You've been studying and

00:01:19.098 --> 00:01:20.366
teaching strategy for

00:01:20.366 --> 00:01:21.370
over 25 years.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.012
Can you share with us how you

00:01:23.012 --> 00:01:24.941
define strategy and explain how

00:01:24.941 --> 00:01:27.730
it's different from planning?

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.930
&gt;&gt; Sure, so, I think the tagline

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.385
here the GSB change lives, change

00:01:32.385 --> 00:01:35.590
organizations, change the world.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.612
What I always like to tell students

00:01:37.612 --> 00:01:39.564
is if you want to change the world,

00:01:39.564 --> 00:01:40.889
you have to build great

00:01:40.889 --> 00:01:42.704
organizations because large scale

00:01:42.704 --> 00:01:44.518
change always happens through

00:01:44.518 --> 00:01:45.579
organizations.

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:46.789
And if you're going to build

00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:47.889
a great organization,

00:01:47.889 --> 00:01:49.374
you have to have the resources to

00:01:49.374 --> 00:01:50.752
sustain that organization.

00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:52.855
And strategy is really about

00:01:52.855 --> 00:01:55.134
explaining how you secure that

00:01:55.134 --> 00:01:58.510
organization's economic prosperity.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:01:59.875
And if you're a public company,

00:01:59.875 --> 00:02:01.094
that means how do you maximize

00:02:01.094 --> 00:02:01.890
profits perhaps.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.726
But even if you're a nonprofit,

00:02:03.726 --> 00:02:05.561
you have to have a strategy that is

00:02:05.561 --> 00:02:07.398
somehow the logic by which you get

00:02:07.398 --> 00:02:09.038
enough resources to be able to

00:02:09.038 --> 00:02:09.694
survive and

00:02:09.694 --> 00:02:11.219
do what it is you want to do.

00:02:11.219 --> 00:02:12.970
Now that's different from planning.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:14.584
Like I think planning is really

00:02:14.584 --> 00:02:16.266
about the order of operations.

00:02:16.266 --> 00:02:18.483
Like it's the sequence of things

00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:20.224
that you're going to do,

00:02:20.224 --> 00:02:22.202
once you know why it is that you

00:02:22.202 --> 00:02:24.339
have a reason to do something and

00:02:24.339 --> 00:02:26.490
what the outcome is going to be.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.056
And so I think strategy

00:02:28.056 --> 00:02:30.530
is that sense much more complicated

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.096
because in order to get

00:02:32.096 --> 00:02:34.404
the resources that you need, you

00:02:34.404 --> 00:02:36.808
have to engage with other actors.

00:02:36.808 --> 00:02:38.283
So you have to engage with your

00:02:38.283 --> 00:02:40.053
customers and your suppliers and so

00:02:40.053 --> 00:02:40.938
on and so forth and

00:02:40.938 --> 00:02:42.714
you don't have control over them.

00:02:42.714 --> 00:02:43.314
Planning is about

00:02:43.314 --> 00:02:44.465
the things that you can control.

00:02:44.465 --> 00:02:46.378
Strategy is really much more

00:02:46.378 --> 00:02:48.825
complicated in that sense.

00:02:48.825 --> 00:02:50.421
&gt;&gt; So it sounds like planning is

00:02:50.421 --> 00:02:51.621
much more tactical.

00:02:51.621 --> 00:02:53.216
Once you have the strategy,

00:02:53.216 --> 00:02:54.744
you then invoke the plans to

00:02:54.744 --> 00:02:56.415
enact that strategy.

00:02:56.415 --> 00:02:57.385
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:02:57.385 --> 00:02:59.450
&gt;&gt; You should that one of the prime

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.039
directives for leaders

00:03:01.039 --> 00:03:04.112
is to focus on building a strategy.

00:03:04.112 --> 00:03:05.710
What are some of the mistakes or

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.308
misconceptions you found over

00:03:07.308 --> 00:03:08.045
the years that

00:03:08.045 --> 00:03:09.643
leaders have about strategy or

00:03:09.643 --> 00:03:11.372
its role in their organization?

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.130
&gt;&gt; So I think the biggest

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.315
misconception that a lot of

00:03:16.315 --> 00:03:19.165
managers in organizations have and

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.635
leaders is that strategy is not

00:03:21.635 --> 00:03:22.585
their job.

00:03:22.585 --> 00:03:23.203
So I think

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:24.981
in a lot of organizations,

00:03:24.981 --> 00:03:27.146
we've had this image of strategy

00:03:27.146 --> 00:03:29.079
being dictated at the top that

00:03:29.079 --> 00:03:31.245
a CEO is going to articulate here's

00:03:31.245 --> 00:03:32.735
what the strategy is.

00:03:32.735 --> 00:03:34.458
And it's like a football coach

00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:35.783
sending the play in from

00:03:35.783 --> 00:03:37.574
the sideline and then we're all

00:03:37.574 --> 00:03:39.109
just going to go and execute.

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:39.875
&gt;&gt; Right. &gt;&gt; And so

00:03:39.875 --> 00:03:42.284
you will meet tons of managers who

00:03:42.284 --> 00:03:44.521
will say I don't do strategy,

00:03:44.521 --> 00:03:46.596
I'm just about execution.

00:03:46.596 --> 00:03:48.602
But of course strategy is really

00:03:48.602 --> 00:03:50.386
about how you're going to best

00:03:50.386 --> 00:03:51.798
allocate your time and

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:52.920
your resources.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.720
That's ultimately what it's about.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:55.727
And so if you have any

00:03:55.727 --> 00:03:57.626
responsibility in an organization,

00:03:57.626 --> 00:03:58.965
you're doing strategy.

00:03:58.965 --> 00:04:00.395
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm. &gt;&gt; And so you need to be

00:04:00.395 --> 00:04:02.330
able to engage with the strategy.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:03.110
And I think

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.277
one of the things that happens

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:07.792
is that we disempower ourselves if

00:04:07.792 --> 00:04:10.230
we think about strategy as that.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.795
The related misconception is then

00:04:12.795 --> 00:04:15.184
that people think of strategy as

00:04:15.184 --> 00:04:17.399
kind of like a set once a year.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:18.169
&gt;&gt; Right. &gt;&gt; Or once every

00:04:18.169 --> 00:04:19.464
five years kind of thing and

00:04:19.464 --> 00:04:21.041
then we're just going to go execute

00:04:21.041 --> 00:04:22.270
on that plan.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.430
But if you think that strategy is

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.830
set no battle plan survives contact

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:27.918
with the- &gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:27.918 --> 00:04:28.616
&gt;&gt; Enemy &gt;&gt; Right?

00:04:28.616 --> 00:04:30.471
&gt;&gt; And so as soon as you start to

00:04:30.471 --> 00:04:32.980
do things, things start to change.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.886
And so it's not just about drafting

00:04:34.886 --> 00:04:36.539
this beautiful architectural

00:04:36.539 --> 00:04:38.380
blueprint and then handing off for

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.223
somebody to go build it because you

00:04:40.223 --> 00:04:41.684
got to adapt to the changing

00:04:41.684 --> 00:04:43.190
landscape.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.191
&gt;&gt; So I'm hearing you say

00:04:44.191 --> 00:04:45.615
that strategy is something that

00:04:45.615 --> 00:04:46.618
everybody owns within

00:04:46.618 --> 00:04:47.470
an organization.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:49.132
It's not just comes down from on

00:04:49.132 --> 00:04:50.733
high and that it has to adapt and

00:04:50.733 --> 00:04:51.235
change.

00:04:51.235 --> 00:04:52.328
I have been part of several

00:04:52.328 --> 00:04:53.869
organizations where when people say

00:04:53.869 --> 00:04:55.459
we're going to strategically plan,

00:04:55.459 --> 00:04:56.561
everybody's eyes roll.

00:04:56.561 --> 00:04:58.089
And they think we're just going to

00:04:58.089 --> 00:04:59.470
put another document on the shelf

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:00.702
or it's just going to sit on some

00:05:00.702 --> 00:05:01.940
websites somewhere.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.255
And what I'm hearing you say is

00:05:03.255 --> 00:05:04.166
that it's living and

00:05:04.166 --> 00:05:05.178
you have to evolve it and

00:05:05.178 --> 00:05:07.320
everybody's involved in it.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.330
Which leads me to the next question

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:11.408
which is in your book and teaching,

00:05:11.408 --> 00:05:13.210
you and Glen Carroll make a very

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.430
convincing claim that organizations

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.990
should craft strategy arguments.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.337
Can you share what you mean by

00:05:19.337 --> 00:05:20.450
a strategy argument?

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.682
What are they and

00:05:21.682 --> 00:05:23.095
how can they help?

00:05:23.095 --> 00:05:25.049
&gt;&gt; What Glenn and I are trying to

00:05:25.049 --> 00:05:27.249
do in that book is basically help

00:05:27.249 --> 00:05:29.611
people think about strategy not as

00:05:29.611 --> 00:05:32.055
just a set of analytical frameworks

00:05:32.055 --> 00:05:33.521
which is what we teach

00:05:33.521 --> 00:05:35.735
many times in business school.

00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:36.834
&gt;&gt; Right. &gt;&gt; So if you think about

00:05:36.834 --> 00:05:38.254
the classic way of teaching

00:05:38.254 --> 00:05:39.920
strategy in a business school is

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.032
porter five forces or

00:05:41.032 --> 00:05:42.947
differentiation versus low cost and

00:05:42.947 --> 00:05:44.693
those are all really important.

00:05:44.693 --> 00:05:46.686
But when you're doing strategy on

00:05:46.686 --> 00:05:48.678
a day-to-day basis, you have to

00:05:48.678 --> 00:05:50.650
understand how is this working?

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:51.801
So it's an explanation and

00:05:51.801 --> 00:05:53.303
that's how I do think it's related

00:05:53.303 --> 00:05:54.160
to communication.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.264
&gt;&gt; Right. &gt;&gt; It's an explanation

00:05:55.264 --> 00:05:56.363
for how you think you're

00:05:56.363 --> 00:05:57.360
going to succeed.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.290
And that's an argument.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.261
And an argument is simply a set of

00:06:00.261 --> 00:06:01.794
assumptions that lead to

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:03.260
a conclusion, right?

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:04.700
That force a conclusion.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.864
And so what a strategy argument is

00:06:06.864 --> 00:06:09.185
it's a statement about what are the

00:06:09.185 --> 00:06:11.584
investments and the activities that

00:06:11.584 --> 00:06:13.750
we are going to undertake that's

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.371
going to lead us to be able to

00:06:15.371 --> 00:06:18.230
create and capture economic value.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:18.971
And if we could do those

00:06:18.971 --> 00:06:19.561
things, right?

00:06:19.561 --> 00:06:21.731
If we can understand the connection

00:06:21.731 --> 00:06:23.613
between those investments and

00:06:23.613 --> 00:06:24.916
those activities and

00:06:24.916 --> 00:06:27.013
how we create economic value, and

00:06:27.013 --> 00:06:28.979
how we capture economic value.

00:06:28.979 --> 00:06:30.387
Then we can start to

00:06:30.387 --> 00:06:33.027
have sophisticated conversations

00:06:33.027 --> 00:06:35.580
about what is it that's working?

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:36.870
What is it that's not working?

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.443
And will give us a model through

00:06:38.443 --> 00:06:40.400
which we can see the world.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.856
&gt;&gt; The thing I feel is so

00:06:41.856 --> 00:06:43.830
powerful about this notion

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:46.316
of strategy as an argument is one,

00:06:46.316 --> 00:06:48.717
it involves how you think about it

00:06:48.717 --> 00:06:51.375
because when we craft arguments, we

00:06:51.375 --> 00:06:53.776
think about things like who are we

00:06:53.776 --> 00:06:56.660
speaking to, what's the context?

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:58.244
And it implies that this is

00:06:58.244 --> 00:07:00.337
a conversation that has to be had.

00:07:00.337 --> 00:07:02.645
Again, it's not some dictation that

00:07:02.645 --> 00:07:04.660
comes down from somebody.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:06.689
So I find that really powerful and

00:07:06.689 --> 00:07:08.791
it also implies in an argument that

00:07:08.791 --> 00:07:10.892
there is debate and discussion and

00:07:10.892 --> 00:07:12.413
change and evolution and

00:07:12.413 --> 00:07:14.595
I think that's really important.

00:07:14.595 --> 00:07:17.007
And as you know I teach strategic

00:07:17.007 --> 00:07:18.973
communication and while I

00:07:18.973 --> 00:07:21.474
talk a lot about the importance of

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:24.333
communicating strategically, I'm

00:07:24.333 --> 00:07:26.745
really curious to have you share

00:07:26.745 --> 00:07:29.425
your thoughts on how communication

00:07:29.425 --> 00:07:32.410
is important in strategy.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:33.782
&gt;&gt; So I think there's two different

00:07:33.782 --> 00:07:34.420
parts of that.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.156
And one just the key author of what

00:07:36.156 --> 00:07:37.892
you just said, like I do think and

00:07:37.892 --> 00:07:39.070
in the book Glen and I,

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.620
we devote an entire chapter to

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:41.984
basically thinking about

00:07:41.984 --> 00:07:43.721
how can you argue constructively

00:07:43.721 --> 00:07:45.560
as a group in an organization.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.489
Often times what we say when we

00:07:47.489 --> 00:07:49.726
teach, if we teach MBA students or

00:07:49.726 --> 00:07:52.248
executives, we say You should go

00:07:52.248 --> 00:07:53.741
and you should argue more in your

00:07:53.741 --> 00:07:55.401
organization's and a lot of people

00:07:55.401 --> 00:07:57.112
are then like, well, whoa, wait,

00:07:57.112 --> 00:07:58.604
I don't want to do that, because

00:07:58.604 --> 00:08:00.172
what they're hearing is fight.

00:08:00.172 --> 00:08:01.952
We're not saying you should go

00:08:01.952 --> 00:08:04.006
fight more, because a fight is kind

00:08:04.006 --> 00:08:05.993
of you're throwing assertions at

00:08:05.993 --> 00:08:08.048
each other and you're just waiting

00:08:08.048 --> 00:08:09.623
for somebody to give in, so

00:08:09.623 --> 00:08:11.213
it's like a street fight.

00:08:11.213 --> 00:08:12.600
But there are ways to

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.049
structure debates and arguments so

00:08:15.049 --> 00:08:17.014
that they are constructed.

00:08:17.014 --> 00:08:19.107
And part of that is about having

00:08:19.107 --> 00:08:21.362
the ability to listen to somebody

00:08:21.362 --> 00:08:23.213
else and hear what it is that

00:08:23.213 --> 00:08:24.984
they're trying to say and

00:08:24.984 --> 00:08:26.770
hear what their logic is.

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:29.265
I think most people believe things

00:08:29.265 --> 00:08:30.297
for a reason,

00:08:30.297 --> 00:08:32.801
they have a reason for believing.

00:08:32.801 --> 00:08:35.039
You might disagree, both with their

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:37.060
conclusion and with some of their

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:39.007
reasons, but you can't engage

00:08:39.007 --> 00:08:41.100
constructively in a debate unless

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.135
you hear them on that dimension.

00:08:43.135 --> 00:08:45.011
And so really, it's about how do

00:08:45.011 --> 00:08:47.088
you structure engagements with each

00:08:47.088 --> 00:08:48.698
other to create that space.

00:08:48.698 --> 00:08:50.198
I think the other part of

00:08:50.198 --> 00:08:52.148
communicating strategically

00:08:52.148 --> 00:08:54.398
that's really important is when we

00:08:54.398 --> 00:08:56.051
think about delegation.

00:08:56.051 --> 00:08:57.667
So, what we know is that if you

00:08:57.667 --> 00:08:59.674
want to build a great organization,

00:08:59.674 --> 00:09:01.291
you're not going to be able to do

00:09:01.291 --> 00:09:02.197
that yourself,

00:09:02.197 --> 00:09:04.010
you can't make all the decisions

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.455
much as you might want to.

00:09:05.455 --> 00:09:06.897
And so we all know that we have to

00:09:06.897 --> 00:09:08.340
be able to delegate, we have to

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:09.625
be able to empower people.

00:09:09.625 --> 00:09:11.515
And we have lots of books that tell

00:09:11.515 --> 00:09:13.271
us delegation is wonderful and

00:09:13.271 --> 00:09:15.296
even the word empowerment sounds so

00:09:15.296 --> 00:09:16.047
wonderful.

00:09:16.047 --> 00:09:18.358
But of course, the truth is most

00:09:18.358 --> 00:09:20.240
leaders wake up at three in

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.979
the morning, petrified because they

00:09:22.979 --> 00:09:25.557
delegated a task to somebody else.

00:09:25.557 --> 00:09:26.698
And what are they petrified?

00:09:26.698 --> 00:09:28.142
What are they scared about?

00:09:28.142 --> 00:09:29.967
They're scared that their

00:09:29.967 --> 00:09:31.712
subordinate is going to make

00:09:31.712 --> 00:09:33.220
the wrong decision and

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:34.885
at three in the morning,

00:09:34.885 --> 00:09:36.800
what is the wrong decision?

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.497
It's not the decision I would have

00:09:39.497 --> 00:09:42.380
made, so there's this tension like

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:44.891
on the one hand you don't want to

00:09:44.891 --> 00:09:47.216
make all the decisions and yet

00:09:47.216 --> 00:09:50.018
you want to make all the decisions.

00:09:50.018 --> 00:09:51.425
And so or at least you want

00:09:51.425 --> 00:09:53.167
somebody who might be closer to

00:09:53.167 --> 00:09:54.976
the information to make a better

00:09:54.976 --> 00:09:56.785
decision because they have more

00:09:56.785 --> 00:09:57.857
information, but

00:09:57.857 --> 00:09:59.532
you want them to think about it

00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:01.083
the way you think about it.

00:10:01.083 --> 00:10:02.579
And then the question is how do you

00:10:02.579 --> 00:10:04.022
get people to think about things

00:10:04.022 --> 00:10:05.473
the way you think about things?

00:10:05.473 --> 00:10:06.609
That's through communication.

00:10:06.609 --> 00:10:10.168
So that's about really explaining

00:10:10.168 --> 00:10:12.307
why you think we win.

00:10:12.307 --> 00:10:14.388
Why you think we are successful

00:10:14.388 --> 00:10:15.511
organization?

00:10:15.511 --> 00:10:17.672
Why you think these sets of actions

00:10:17.672 --> 00:10:19.608
are the things that we need to do

00:10:19.608 --> 00:10:21.781
in order to accomplish our goals?

00:10:21.781 --> 00:10:23.545
And so that's where I do think

00:10:23.545 --> 00:10:25.381
really clear communication is

00:10:25.381 --> 00:10:27.288
going to be essential to effective

00:10:27.288 --> 00:10:28.777
strategic leadership.

00:10:28.777 --> 00:10:30.788
&gt;&gt; The fact that you are invoking

00:10:30.788 --> 00:10:32.959
the power of listening and being

00:10:32.959 --> 00:10:35.373
willing to engage in conflict, but

00:10:35.373 --> 00:10:37.787
productive conflict, so we can get

00:10:37.787 --> 00:10:40.039
to a collaborative place with our

00:10:40.039 --> 00:10:42.372
strategy is a very different way of

00:10:42.372 --> 00:10:44.965
thinking about strategy in general.

00:10:44.965 --> 00:10:46.754
And I think that's really powerful

00:10:46.754 --> 00:10:48.191
for all of us to think about.

00:10:48.191 --> 00:10:49.720
And clearly, being able to

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:51.915
articulate your strategy, packaging

00:10:51.915 --> 00:10:53.776
it, and communicating it in a way

00:10:53.776 --> 00:10:55.704
that not only motivates people but

00:10:55.704 --> 00:10:57.699
also helps make better decisions is

00:10:57.699 --> 00:10:59.044
absolutely critical.

00:10:59.044 --> 00:11:00.656
&gt;&gt; Right, I mean, there's this

00:11:00.656 --> 00:11:02.330
element of strategy that I think

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:03.880
most of our stereotype of what

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.564
strategy is, is very top down.

00:11:05.564 --> 00:11:06.269
&gt;&gt; Yes. &gt;&gt; And so

00:11:06.269 --> 00:11:07.497
then you would think

00:11:07.497 --> 00:11:09.962
communication's just broadcasting.

00:11:09.962 --> 00:11:10.716
But of course,

00:11:10.716 --> 00:11:12.166
we also know that lots of great

00:11:12.166 --> 00:11:13.792
strategies are discovered, and

00:11:13.792 --> 00:11:15.244
they're discovered because

00:11:15.244 --> 00:11:16.752
the leaders were able to listen

00:11:16.752 --> 00:11:18.203
to their frontline workers or

00:11:18.203 --> 00:11:19.945
their frontline managers tell them

00:11:19.945 --> 00:11:21.860
here's something that's even better

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.276
than what we're doing now.

00:11:23.276 --> 00:11:24.260
And so of course,

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.705
strategy is really both,

00:11:25.705 --> 00:11:27.620
it's both top down and bottom up.

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:29.336
And so you have to be able to both

00:11:29.336 --> 00:11:31.118
being able to articulate what it

00:11:31.118 --> 00:11:32.438
is that you want to say and

00:11:32.438 --> 00:11:34.418
where you think we should be going,

00:11:34.418 --> 00:11:36.266
but then also be able to listen and

00:11:36.266 --> 00:11:37.986
hear where you might be wrong.

00:11:37.986 --> 00:11:39.619
because I think the other part that

00:11:39.619 --> 00:11:40.990
we really emphasize is in

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:42.164
an argument, well,

00:11:42.164 --> 00:11:43.749
how do you make an argument?

00:11:43.749 --> 00:11:45.250
You make assumptions.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.037
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:11:46.037 --> 00:11:47.797
&gt;&gt; And assumptions are testable,

00:11:47.797 --> 00:11:49.622
they're going to be proven wrong or

00:11:49.622 --> 00:11:51.455
they're going to be proven right.

00:11:51.455 --> 00:11:52.588
And you have to be open to

00:11:52.588 --> 00:11:54.348
the possibility that the assumption

00:11:54.348 --> 00:11:55.936
you made yesterday has turned out

00:11:55.936 --> 00:11:56.853
to be wrong today.

00:11:56.853 --> 00:11:58.217
And that you therefore need

00:11:58.217 --> 00:12:00.114
to revise your theory, that doesn't

00:12:00.114 --> 00:12:01.417
mean you have to go back and

00:12:01.417 --> 00:12:02.068
give up, but

00:12:02.068 --> 00:12:03.608
it means that you have to update

00:12:03.608 --> 00:12:05.347
your understanding of the world.

00:12:05.347 --> 00:12:07.544
We've talked a lot about testing

00:12:07.544 --> 00:12:08.643
assumptions and

00:12:08.643 --> 00:12:10.998
how to really challenge yourself to

00:12:10.998 --> 00:12:12.725
be open to what the reality

00:12:12.725 --> 00:12:14.531
is instead of staying fixed

00:12:14.531 --> 00:12:16.350
on what you thought it was.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:17.624
And thank you for

00:12:17.624 --> 00:12:19.353
re emphasizing that.

00:12:19.353 --> 00:12:20.396
I have to tell you,

00:12:20.396 --> 00:12:21.962
when I was doing research for

00:12:21.962 --> 00:12:23.920
our conversation, I had a big smile

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.615
on my face when I saw that in your

00:12:25.615 --> 00:12:27.376
class and in your thinking about

00:12:27.376 --> 00:12:29.009
strategic communication and

00:12:29.009 --> 00:12:30.836
communicating about strategy,

00:12:30.836 --> 00:12:32.664
you talk a lot about how important

00:12:32.664 --> 00:12:33.734
storytelling is.

00:12:33.734 --> 00:12:36.285
And I'm curious if you can help us

00:12:36.285 --> 00:12:38.930
understand what makes a good story

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:40.305
about strategy.

00:12:40.305 --> 00:12:42.655
&gt;&gt; So if you think about strategy

00:12:42.655 --> 00:12:45.005
as an argument, then I think a

00:12:45.005 --> 00:12:48.013
natural criterion suggests itself,

00:12:48.013 --> 00:12:50.366
and that criterion is logic.

00:12:50.366 --> 00:12:51.894
So a good strategy story,

00:12:51.894 --> 00:12:52.866
on the one hand,

00:12:52.866 --> 00:12:54.740
it has a clear narrative to it and

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.336
you can follow the steps and

00:12:56.336 --> 00:12:57.460
so on and so forth.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:59.654
But it should also be internally

00:12:59.654 --> 00:13:00.710
coherent, and

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:02.586
that is the realm of logic.

00:13:02.586 --> 00:13:04.880
And philosophers and logicians have

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:07.100
spent centuries thinking about how

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.172
do we assess arguments in terms of

00:13:09.172 --> 00:13:10.504
whether or not, like

00:13:10.504 --> 00:13:13.020
whatever assumptions you're making,

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.500
do the assumptions that

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.572
you do make lead to the conclusion

00:13:16.572 --> 00:13:18.066
that you think it does?

00:13:18.066 --> 00:13:19.941
And that is I think incredibly

00:13:19.941 --> 00:13:22.041
important in assessing strategy

00:13:22.041 --> 00:13:22.721
stories.

00:13:22.721 --> 00:13:24.622
And where it's at is particularly

00:13:24.622 --> 00:13:26.392
important is when you're doing

00:13:26.392 --> 00:13:28.357
strategy for the future, like when

00:13:28.357 --> 00:13:30.323
you're trying to say here's where

00:13:30.323 --> 00:13:31.843
we want to be in five years.

00:13:31.843 --> 00:13:33.436
Here's my narrative.

00:13:33.436 --> 00:13:34.621
Well, think about it,

00:13:34.621 --> 00:13:36.106
you're telling a story about

00:13:36.106 --> 00:13:38.072
something that hasn't happened yet.

00:13:38.072 --> 00:13:39.249
And so how do you know whether

00:13:39.249 --> 00:13:40.186
that's a good story?

00:13:40.186 --> 00:13:42.355
You can say, well, it resonates

00:13:42.355 --> 00:13:44.526
with me, it inspires me and those

00:13:44.526 --> 00:13:47.018
are important than useful criteria.

00:13:47.018 --> 00:13:47.916
But of course,

00:13:47.916 --> 00:13:49.854
they could also be foolishness.

00:13:49.854 --> 00:13:51.560
So you want to also be able to say

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.610
okay I don't know whether all these

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.455
assumptions are going to be true

00:13:55.455 --> 00:13:57.179
and I can't know until I act.

00:13:57.179 --> 00:13:58.670
But at least I want to know

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:00.459
whether, if they are true,

00:14:00.459 --> 00:14:02.248
will they lead to the outcome

00:14:02.248 --> 00:14:04.202
that I think they will lead to.

00:14:04.202 --> 00:14:05.731
And that's why in the book,

00:14:05.731 --> 00:14:06.750
glad when I get into

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:08.472
this a somewhat technical set of

00:14:08.472 --> 00:14:10.322
choppers on thinking about what is

00:14:10.322 --> 00:14:12.237
logical validity and soundness and

00:14:12.237 --> 00:14:13.956
so on and so forth, which I think

00:14:13.956 --> 00:14:15.805
a lot of people were doing strategy

00:14:15.805 --> 00:14:17.378
or where did that come from?

00:14:17.378 --> 00:14:18.752
And I stopped thinking about that

00:14:18.752 --> 00:14:19.586
in eighth grade, or

00:14:19.586 --> 00:14:20.525
something like that.

00:14:20.525 --> 00:14:22.033
But I do think that holding

00:14:22.033 --> 00:14:23.952
on to those ideas is like, well,

00:14:23.952 --> 00:14:25.599
the way we should assess each

00:14:25.599 --> 00:14:27.452
other's arguments in the first

00:14:27.452 --> 00:14:29.373
instance is by the extent to which

00:14:29.373 --> 00:14:31.310
they're internally coherent.

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:31.907
&gt;&gt; Right, and

00:14:31.907 --> 00:14:33.279
one of the things I strongly

00:14:33.279 --> 00:14:34.952
advocated that whenever you're

00:14:34.952 --> 00:14:36.683
creating content stories, etc,

00:14:36.683 --> 00:14:38.474
that there is a logical coherence,

00:14:38.474 --> 00:14:40.026
things follow, and that their

00:14:40.026 --> 00:14:41.776
premises that you could support.

00:14:41.776 --> 00:14:43.319
I'm curious to get another

00:14:43.319 --> 00:14:44.729
dimension of story that I

00:14:44.729 --> 00:14:46.675
wonder your thoughts on which is,

00:14:46.675 --> 00:14:48.619
once you have a strategy, you have

00:14:48.619 --> 00:14:50.718
to propagate it in an organization.

00:14:50.718 --> 00:14:52.386
And sometimes I think the most

00:14:52.386 --> 00:14:54.053
effective way to do that is not

00:14:54.053 --> 00:14:55.386
putting it on posters or

00:14:55.386 --> 00:14:57.189
little note cards people have,

00:14:57.189 --> 00:14:59.340
it's the stories that leaders tell.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:01.276
Do you have experience on that or

00:15:01.276 --> 00:15:02.925
thoughts on how once we have

00:15:02.925 --> 00:15:04.932
the strategy, how do we actually

00:15:04.932 --> 00:15:06.080
communicate it and

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:07.945
use story to actually motivate

00:15:07.945 --> 00:15:10.043
people to align to that strategy?

00:15:10.043 --> 00:15:11.526
&gt;&gt; I think, first of all,

00:15:11.526 --> 00:15:13.679
I totally agree with the premise of

00:15:13.679 --> 00:15:15.831
your question, but I do think that

00:15:15.831 --> 00:15:17.836
communication of these stories

00:15:17.836 --> 00:15:19.990
is really important and I would add

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:21.867
relentless communication.

00:15:21.867 --> 00:15:24.975
I think one aspect of that is it's

00:15:24.975 --> 00:15:28.305
very easy to approach management of

00:15:28.305 --> 00:15:31.524
leadership as a exercise in giving

00:15:31.524 --> 00:15:32.416
orders.

00:15:32.416 --> 00:15:33.726
And I think that's fine,

00:15:33.726 --> 00:15:35.274
I think there is an element to it

00:15:35.274 --> 00:15:37.181
that's about getting delegation and

00:15:37.181 --> 00:15:38.027
giving orders.

00:15:38.027 --> 00:15:40.523
But maybe give people a little bit

00:15:40.523 --> 00:15:43.475
of grace in the sense of saying.

00:15:43.475 --> 00:15:44.419
Here's why.

00:15:44.419 --> 00:15:46.303
Here's what I'm thinking, and

00:15:46.303 --> 00:15:48.187
just let people into your mind and

00:15:48.187 --> 00:15:50.071
your logic because then they're

00:15:50.071 --> 00:15:51.360
going to do a better job.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:53.148
I just think that's the most

00:15:53.148 --> 00:15:55.160
important thing, and so I think

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.096
the more you can just be aware of

00:15:57.096 --> 00:15:59.331
the need to always be communicating

00:15:59.331 --> 00:16:00.970
what your reasoning is and

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:02.237
what your logic is,

00:16:02.237 --> 00:16:03.969
the better off you'll be.

00:16:03.969 --> 00:16:05.676
&gt;&gt; I absolutely agree, and if

00:16:05.676 --> 00:16:07.979
people can internalize your logic,

00:16:07.979 --> 00:16:09.909
they can then be able to not only

00:16:09.909 --> 00:16:11.616
know what they need to do and

00:16:11.616 --> 00:16:12.879
feel confident that

00:16:12.879 --> 00:16:15.180
it's the right thing, they can then

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:16.909
communicate it to others.

00:16:16.909 --> 00:16:18.913
So it becomes a way of propagating

00:16:18.913 --> 00:16:20.135
that information.

00:16:20.135 --> 00:16:21.499
&gt;&gt; And they can more productively

00:16:21.499 --> 00:16:22.393
disagree with you.

00:16:22.393 --> 00:16:23.058
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:16:23.058 --> 00:16:24.220
&gt;&gt; Right, because then they can

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:25.846
say, but wait a second, you thought

00:16:25.846 --> 00:16:27.007
that this was going to be true and

00:16:27.007 --> 00:16:27.982
it's not, so now boss,

00:16:27.982 --> 00:16:29.442
we gotta do something differently.

00:16:29.442 --> 00:16:30.207
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah. &gt;&gt; And it's

00:16:30.207 --> 00:16:31.860
disrespectful or anything like

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:33.695
that, it's just the world wasn't

00:16:33.695 --> 00:16:35.160
the way you thought it was and

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:36.703
I can help you do a better job.

00:16:36.703 --> 00:16:37.913
&gt;&gt; Right, and it allows for

00:16:37.913 --> 00:16:39.584
that immediate feedback so you can

00:16:39.584 --> 00:16:41.261
make those adjustments for sure.

00:16:41.261 --> 00:16:42.242
Before we end,

00:16:42.242 --> 00:16:44.355
I'd like to ask you the same three

00:16:44.355 --> 00:16:46.100
questions I ask everyone.

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:46.930
You okay with that?

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:48.772
&gt;&gt; Yep. &gt;&gt; All right, here we go.

00:16:48.772 --> 00:16:50.915
If you were to capture the best

00:16:50.915 --> 00:16:53.491
communication advice you've ever

00:16:53.491 --> 00:16:55.720
received as a five to seven word

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.780
presentation slide title,

00:16:57.780 --> 00:16:59.256
what would that be?

00:16:59.256 --> 00:17:01.570
&gt;&gt; The five to seven words would be

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:03.529
a classroom is a theater.

00:17:03.529 --> 00:17:05.602
So I had the pleasure of working

00:17:05.602 --> 00:17:07.676
a number of times actually with

00:17:07.676 --> 00:17:09.192
a teaching coach named

00:17:09.192 --> 00:17:10.803
Barbara Elaine Brown.

00:17:10.803 --> 00:17:14.036
And she was a former actor and

00:17:14.036 --> 00:17:16.333
theatre director.

00:17:16.333 --> 00:17:18.477
And she really helped me think

00:17:18.477 --> 00:17:20.794
about how when you're teaching

00:17:20.794 --> 00:17:23.280
a class, which is obviously a very

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:25.597
strong form of communication,

00:17:25.597 --> 00:17:27.655
you're putting on a play and

00:17:27.655 --> 00:17:29.455
you have to think about it

00:17:29.455 --> 00:17:30.515
the same way.

00:17:30.515 --> 00:17:32.599
So when you're first starting out,

00:17:32.599 --> 00:17:34.347
for example, you kind of tend to

00:17:34.347 --> 00:17:35.691
just kind of [SOUND] okay,

00:17:35.691 --> 00:17:37.249
let me just start talking.

00:17:37.249 --> 00:17:38.080
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:39.309
&gt;&gt; As opposed to taking command

00:17:39.309 --> 00:17:39.875
of the room.

00:17:39.875 --> 00:17:41.275
A play doesn't start by somebody

00:17:41.275 --> 00:17:42.575
just wandering out on stage and

00:17:42.575 --> 00:17:43.429
deciding to start.

00:17:43.429 --> 00:17:44.072
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well,

00:17:44.072 --> 00:17:45.184
a good play doesn't.

00:17:45.184 --> 00:17:46.247
&gt;&gt; A good play doesn't.

00:17:46.247 --> 00:17:49.129
And a good theater performance has

00:17:49.129 --> 00:17:51.221
different characters.

00:17:51.221 --> 00:17:52.876
And so thinking about yourself as

00:17:52.876 --> 00:17:53.999
an individual and like

00:17:53.999 --> 00:17:55.891
the different characters that you'd

00:17:55.891 --> 00:17:57.427
like to deploy in the same class

00:17:57.427 --> 00:17:59.201
and thinking about engagement with

00:17:59.201 --> 00:18:00.795
the audience and how you move and

00:18:00.795 --> 00:18:02.469
all those other kinds of things.

00:18:02.469 --> 00:18:04.445
It was really powerful for me,

00:18:04.445 --> 00:18:06.345
it changed the way I taught and

00:18:06.345 --> 00:18:08.171
definitely for the better.

00:18:08.171 --> 00:18:09.575
&gt;&gt; I have to say that I'm

00:18:09.575 --> 00:18:11.291
pleasantly surprised and

00:18:11.291 --> 00:18:12.309
a bit shocked.

00:18:12.309 --> 00:18:13.509
I see you as somebody who is,

00:18:13.509 --> 00:18:15.159
I mean, you're a fantastic teacher,

00:18:15.159 --> 00:18:16.359
and you're very logical and

00:18:16.359 --> 00:18:16.963
methodical.

00:18:16.963 --> 00:18:18.730
And I love that I now see behind

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:20.288
the curtain literally and

00:18:20.288 --> 00:18:22.342
figuratively that you think about

00:18:22.342 --> 00:18:23.136
it as a play.

00:18:23.136 --> 00:18:24.718
And I think all of us should think

00:18:24.718 --> 00:18:25.891
about the theater of our

00:18:25.891 --> 00:18:26.778
communication.

00:18:26.778 --> 00:18:28.530
Now I don't mean we have to be

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.501
actors, but think about the way

00:18:30.501 --> 00:18:32.044
the environment set up.

00:18:32.044 --> 00:18:33.109
How are you starting,

00:18:33.109 --> 00:18:34.734
as you mentioned, how do you end,

00:18:34.734 --> 00:18:36.303
how do you build excitement, and

00:18:36.303 --> 00:18:37.872
all of those are things that we can

00:18:37.872 --> 00:18:38.842
learn from acting.

00:18:38.842 --> 00:18:41.616
And thank you, that's fantastic.

00:18:41.616 --> 00:18:43.948
Who is a communicator that you

00:18:43.948 --> 00:18:45.443
admire, and why?

00:18:45.443 --> 00:18:46.736
&gt;&gt; So having listened to your

00:18:46.736 --> 00:18:48.440
podcast, I of course knew you're

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:49.745
going to ask this question.

00:18:49.745 --> 00:18:50.319
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yes.

00:18:50.319 --> 00:18:51.802
&gt;&gt; I'm in general terrible at

00:18:51.802 --> 00:18:53.609
answering questions like who's

00:18:53.609 --> 00:18:54.645
your favorite ex?

00:18:54.645 --> 00:18:55.384
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry.

00:18:55.384 --> 00:18:56.324
&gt;&gt; And so I was stopped.

00:18:56.324 --> 00:18:57.141
No, no, no, that's fine.

00:18:57.141 --> 00:18:58.492
So I texted my kids,

00:18:58.492 --> 00:19:00.795
my kids are all grown, and I said,

00:19:00.795 --> 00:19:03.190
how should I answer this question?

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:05.199
And one of my sons said,

00:19:05.199 --> 00:19:06.811
build Belichick.

00:19:06.811 --> 00:19:08.018
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; because I'm a big

00:19:08.018 --> 00:19:09.433
New England Patriots fan.

00:19:09.433 --> 00:19:10.823
And then my other son said,

00:19:10.823 --> 00:19:11.791
the Swedish Chef,

00:19:11.791 --> 00:19:12.826
because he's a moppet.

00:19:12.826 --> 00:19:14.322
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And

00:19:14.322 --> 00:19:16.101
then my daughter said, Emmett,

00:19:16.101 --> 00:19:17.314
who is one of our dogs.

00:19:17.314 --> 00:19:19.839
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And I thought about

00:19:19.839 --> 00:19:21.396
because what Emmett the dog does,

00:19:21.396 --> 00:19:23.141
he has this amazing ability to get

00:19:23.141 --> 00:19:25.010
me up with nonverbal communication

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:26.520
at 5.30 in the morning.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:27.233
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; It's just his

00:19:27.233 --> 00:19:28.476
ability. So I actually thought,

00:19:28.476 --> 00:19:29.815
well, what unifies all of those?

00:19:29.815 --> 00:19:31.384
because think about Bill Belichick,

00:19:31.384 --> 00:19:32.953
most people would not think of him

00:19:32.953 --> 00:19:34.580
as a great communicator because he

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:36.151
very intentionally, actually I

00:19:36.151 --> 00:19:37.566
think is not communicating.

00:19:37.566 --> 00:19:38.858
He doesn't want to say anything.

00:19:38.858 --> 00:19:40.366
And so I actually thought, well,

00:19:40.366 --> 00:19:41.718
while I was trying to make sense

00:19:41.718 --> 00:19:43.122
of that and why would they all say

00:19:43.122 --> 00:19:44.422
that as a response to what they

00:19:44.422 --> 00:19:45.625
thought I would love to be.

00:19:45.625 --> 00:19:46.782
And I think it actually has

00:19:46.782 --> 00:19:48.150
something to do with nonverbal

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:48.948
communication.

00:19:48.948 --> 00:19:49.771
And so then I thought

00:19:49.771 --> 00:19:50.501
about some more.

00:19:50.501 --> 00:19:51.922
I actually think I,

00:19:51.922 --> 00:19:54.265
one of the musicians that I really

00:19:54.265 --> 00:19:56.524
have always enjoyed and loved is

00:19:56.524 --> 00:19:59.131
a jazz pianist named Keith Jarrett.

00:19:59.131 --> 00:20:01.147
Who has this amazing style of

00:20:01.147 --> 00:20:02.911
performance and again,

00:20:02.911 --> 00:20:04.759
entirely nonverbal, but

00:20:04.759 --> 00:20:06.943
if you think about the way great

00:20:06.943 --> 00:20:09.463
musicians use pacing and rhythm and

00:20:09.463 --> 00:20:11.479
kind of the ability to create

00:20:11.479 --> 00:20:14.182
drama without ever saying anything.

00:20:14.182 --> 00:20:15.269
because I think what I find hard

00:20:15.269 --> 00:20:16.209
about this question,

00:20:16.209 --> 00:20:17.257
it's a great question.

00:20:17.257 --> 00:20:20.220
What I find hard about it is, it's

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.878
so hard to separate the message

00:20:22.878 --> 00:20:25.650
from the communication style.

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:27.406
So that's where I think the music

00:20:27.406 --> 00:20:29.413
helps me think about, okay, I don't

00:20:29.413 --> 00:20:31.232
really know what the message is but

00:20:31.232 --> 00:20:32.987
I know how it moves me in different

00:20:32.987 --> 00:20:33.819
kinds of ways.

00:20:33.819 --> 00:20:35.250
&gt;&gt; Well, first I am glad that I had

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:36.904
an opportunity to bring your family

00:20:36.904 --> 00:20:37.464
together.

00:20:37.464 --> 00:20:38.442
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:20:38.442 --> 00:20:39.596
&gt;&gt; I was very curious how you were

00:20:39.596 --> 00:20:40.751
going to have a football coach,

00:20:40.751 --> 00:20:42.346
a moppet- &gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And

00:20:42.346 --> 00:20:44.558
a pet lead to something.

00:20:44.558 --> 00:20:46.024
But you're right, nonverbal

00:20:46.024 --> 00:20:47.489
communication is critical and

00:20:47.489 --> 00:20:49.065
you were one of the very few people

00:20:49.065 --> 00:20:50.698
to answer this question focused on

00:20:50.698 --> 00:20:51.389
nonverbals.

00:20:51.389 --> 00:20:53.558
Most people speak about people who

00:20:53.558 --> 00:20:54.605
communicate and

00:20:54.605 --> 00:20:56.106
speak in a certain way.

00:20:56.106 --> 00:20:57.730
Question number three,

00:20:57.730 --> 00:20:59.277
what are the first three

00:20:59.277 --> 00:21:00.900
ingredients that go into

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:03.382
a successful communication recipe?

00:21:03.382 --> 00:21:04.746
&gt;&gt; My first ingredient is not

00:21:04.746 --> 00:21:06.358
going to surprise, given what we

00:21:06.358 --> 00:21:07.972
have just been talking about.

00:21:07.972 --> 00:21:09.646
But I think the first ingredient is

00:21:09.646 --> 00:21:11.262
clarify your logic, so be really

00:21:11.262 --> 00:21:13.005
clear about what your argument is.

00:21:13.005 --> 00:21:15.119
And that saying that clear thinking

00:21:15.119 --> 00:21:17.021
is what leads to clear writing or

00:21:17.021 --> 00:21:18.861
clear speaking is really key.

00:21:18.861 --> 00:21:20.656
And when I'm oftentimes giving

00:21:20.656 --> 00:21:22.518
feedback on papers to students or

00:21:22.518 --> 00:21:24.512
whatever, it's usually about the

00:21:24.512 --> 00:21:26.240
writing is a mess until they get

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:28.300
to the point where they're actually

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:30.294
clear about what they're trying to

00:21:30.294 --> 00:21:32.178
say, which is oftentimes hard.

00:21:32.178 --> 00:21:33.233
So that's not a criticism, but

00:21:33.233 --> 00:21:33.980
it's just like that.

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:35.262
&gt;&gt; Right. &gt;&gt; So that's the process.

00:21:35.262 --> 00:21:35.940
And related

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:37.843
to that is writing is rewriting.

00:21:37.843 --> 00:21:39.482
You have to keep doing, and what

00:21:39.482 --> 00:21:41.246
you're doing is you're revising

00:21:41.246 --> 00:21:42.839
your argument as you go along.

00:21:42.839 --> 00:21:45.985
Second ingredient I think is

00:21:45.985 --> 00:21:49.524
imagine what the audience might

00:21:49.524 --> 00:21:52.810
hear, not just what you say.

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:54.662
I think we focus very much on

00:21:54.662 --> 00:21:56.517
getting the words right but

00:21:56.517 --> 00:21:58.856
not enough on what the audience's

00:21:58.856 --> 00:22:00.872
perspective is going to be and

00:22:00.872 --> 00:22:02.888
how they're going to interpret

00:22:02.888 --> 00:22:03.711
the words.

00:22:03.711 --> 00:22:06.447
And I think one of the ways that I

00:22:06.447 --> 00:22:09.394
got better as a case class teacher

00:22:09.394 --> 00:22:10.551
was to be able

00:22:10.551 --> 00:22:13.605
to anticipate better how students

00:22:13.605 --> 00:22:16.674
were going to interpret a question.

00:22:16.674 --> 00:22:18.712
All the different ways they could

00:22:18.712 --> 00:22:19.876
interpret it, and

00:22:19.876 --> 00:22:21.562
that I think is really key.

00:22:21.562 --> 00:22:23.228
And then I think the last

00:22:23.228 --> 00:22:25.394
ingredient I would say is be all

00:22:25.394 --> 00:22:26.817
parts of yourself.

00:22:26.817 --> 00:22:28.665
Which goes back to the this idea of

00:22:28.665 --> 00:22:30.315
the classroom as a theater and

00:22:30.315 --> 00:22:32.163
that you actually have within you

00:22:32.163 --> 00:22:34.143
many different characters that you

00:22:34.143 --> 00:22:35.729
deploy at different times.

00:22:35.729 --> 00:22:37.181
The stern person and

00:22:37.181 --> 00:22:39.488
the joking person, and that you

00:22:39.488 --> 00:22:42.052
shouldn't be afraid of using all of

00:22:42.052 --> 00:22:44.531
them at the appropriate moments in

00:22:44.531 --> 00:22:46.852
any given communication task.

00:22:46.852 --> 00:22:48.232
&gt;&gt; I really like that notion of

00:22:48.232 --> 00:22:49.672
bringing your whole self and

00:22:49.672 --> 00:22:51.352
giving yourself permission to be

00:22:51.352 --> 00:22:53.098
who you are when you communicate.

00:22:53.098 --> 00:22:54.076
That's very powerful.

00:22:54.076 --> 00:22:54.850
I feel a lot

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:57.000
of us put on this comunication

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:59.151
person instead of who we are.

00:22:59.151 --> 00:23:00.987
And that notion of really thinking

00:23:00.987 --> 00:23:02.696
about the impact of your words on

00:23:02.696 --> 00:23:04.343
your audience and planning for

00:23:04.343 --> 00:23:05.671
some of that in advance,

00:23:05.671 --> 00:23:07.443
I think speaks very nicely to your

00:23:07.443 --> 00:23:08.670
strategic thinking.

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:11.199
And clearly, logic is critical.

00:23:11.199 --> 00:23:12.648
We've all heard people who

00:23:12.648 --> 00:23:14.349
just ramble and we wonder why and

00:23:14.349 --> 00:23:15.360
what makes sense.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.397
Well, Yasper, thank you so much for

00:23:17.397 --> 00:23:18.127
joining us.

00:23:18.127 --> 00:23:19.791
You made quite a convincing

00:23:19.791 --> 00:23:21.238
strategic argument for

00:23:21.238 --> 00:23:22.690
the value of strategy.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:23.431
And further,

00:23:23.431 --> 00:23:24.975
you helped us understand what

00:23:24.975 --> 00:23:26.643
makes for good strategy and good

00:23:26.643 --> 00:23:28.570
communication as well, thank you.

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:29.469
&gt;&gt; Thank you for having me.

00:23:29.469 --> 00:23:39.469
[MUSIC]

